1
00:00:32,206 --> 00:00:35,078
The 1990s were a golden era

2
00:00:35,122 --> 00:00:36,558
for The Walt Disney Company.

3
00:00:36,558 --> 00:00:37,907
The Little Mermaid 
revived the company

4
00:00:37,907 --> 00:00:39,996
from the edge of bankruptcy.

5
00:00:39,996 --> 00:00:43,086
Beauty and the Beast earned
a Best Picture nomination.

6
00:00:43,130 --> 00:00:46,785
And The Lion King broke
box office records worldwide.

7
00:00:48,657 --> 00:00:53,357
But the reality just miles away
was no fairy tale.

8
00:00:53,357 --> 00:00:57,796
In 1992, a state jury acquitted

9
00:00:57,883 --> 00:01:01,670
four LAPD officers
on almost all charges

10
00:01:01,713 --> 00:01:03,628
in the beating
of Rodney G. King.

11
00:01:05,674 --> 00:01:09,373
The response was anger.

12
00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:13,029
Fire, chaos,

13
00:01:13,029 --> 00:01:16,337
massive citywide destruction.

14
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:20,254
It was a pivotal moment
for Black culture in the '90s.

15
00:01:20,341 --> 00:01:23,953
But it's a little-known fact
that another cultural milestone

16
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:26,390
emerged from that fateful day.

17
00:01:28,479 --> 00:01:31,265
It's a story so unbelievable,

18
00:01:31,352 --> 00:01:33,745
it could only happen
in Hollywood.

19
00:01:35,051 --> 00:01:38,837
How did this young,
Black, unassuming animator

20
00:01:38,837 --> 00:01:42,841
from East Atlanta wind up

21
00:01:42,841 --> 00:01:45,627
as the CEO of Disney

22
00:01:45,627 --> 00:01:49,544
at the company's most powerful
moment in time?

23
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:15,222
- Okay, rolling.
- Speed.

24
00:02:15,309 --> 00:02:17,093
Believe it or not,

25
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:18,964
we named him after Tom Jones.

26
00:02:19,008 --> 00:02:23,230
♪

27
00:02:23,230 --> 00:02:25,188
Yeah!

28
00:02:25,232 --> 00:02:28,626
His father
used to play Tom Jones.

29
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:31,673
That's how he first courted me.

30
00:02:31,673 --> 00:02:35,285
He would do a little dance
and we would laugh.

31
00:02:35,329 --> 00:02:37,418
♪ I got to tell you a story♪

32
00:02:37,461 --> 00:02:39,420
♪ Every man ought to know...♪

33
00:02:39,463 --> 00:02:41,248
Usually, the boys
in Ronald's family

34
00:02:41,248 --> 00:02:44,120
was named after their fathers,

35
00:02:44,207 --> 00:02:46,905
but when we had our boy,
we knew that

36
00:02:46,992 --> 00:02:48,429
he was gonna be different,

37
00:02:48,472 --> 00:02:50,474
so we had to do something different.

38
00:02:50,474 --> 00:02:54,261
We had to name him Thomas.

39
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:57,873
T loved cartoons
something crazy.

40
00:02:57,916 --> 00:02:59,788
I mean, he was always drawing
on his desk, uh,

41
00:02:59,875 --> 00:03:02,878
in, uh, in the margins of
his notebook, in the textbooks,

42
00:03:02,921 --> 00:03:05,228
all on the walls, man.

43
00:03:05,272 --> 00:03:06,925
He just drove
our teachers crazy.

44
00:03:07,012 --> 00:03:11,713
He was obsessed with this
one cat named, um, Blasto Boy?

45
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:14,281
No, no, no, Astro Boy.

46
00:03:19,503 --> 00:03:21,940
Yeah, yeah, I used to
watch it with him,

47
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,159
and I remember thinking...

48
00:03:23,203 --> 00:03:26,945
this is kind of boring.

49
00:03:26,945 --> 00:03:28,295
But Thomas loved it, man.

50
00:03:28,382 --> 00:03:30,514
He drew... he drew
that dude everywhere.

51
00:03:30,601 --> 00:03:33,735
One time, he asked me, he said,

52
00:03:33,735 --> 00:03:37,173
"What if Astro Boy
came here and saved us?"

53
00:03:37,173 --> 00:03:39,610
I said, "Saved us from what?"

54
00:03:39,697 --> 00:03:44,572
And he just kind of shrugged
and said, "Everything."

55
00:03:48,967 --> 00:03:50,926
I mean, what kind
of eight-year-old

56
00:03:50,969 --> 00:03:53,189
is thinking like that,
you know?

57
00:03:53,189 --> 00:03:54,538
I mean, I think he was scared.

58
00:03:54,625 --> 00:03:56,845
He wasn't no punk,
I'm just saying, I don't

59
00:03:56,845 --> 00:03:59,761
think he ever understood
why the world was so unfair.

60
00:03:59,761 --> 00:04:01,545
And where we were growing up...

61
00:04:01,632 --> 00:04:04,200
it was, like, 
unfair unfair, you know?

62
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,289
I mean, you could be
sitting at home watching

63
00:04:06,333 --> 00:04:08,552
a program with white folks
with nice houses.

64
00:04:08,552 --> 00:04:09,771
We couldn't even
ride ours bikes outside,

65
00:04:09,771 --> 00:04:11,207
cause niggas
will take it from you.

66
00:04:11,294 --> 00:04:14,645
Straight to your face,
they do not care. Crime?

67
00:04:14,645 --> 00:04:16,343
What, so? They will
ride your bike in front

68
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:17,474
of your house the next day.

69
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:21,565
Yeah, unfair.

70
00:04:21,565 --> 00:04:23,437
The boys picked on him.

71
00:04:23,524 --> 00:04:26,570
He would come home
sometimes with pages

72
00:04:26,657 --> 00:04:28,355
torn out of
his little sketchbook.

73
00:04:28,355 --> 00:04:30,444
Yeah, we would call him white.

74
00:04:30,487 --> 00:04:31,880
We was just messing with him.

75
00:04:31,923 --> 00:04:33,316
Um, I think he was
just trying to fit in,

76
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:35,753
but he wasn't really like us,
you know?

77
00:04:35,797 --> 00:04:37,233
And the kids
would call him on it.

78
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:40,236
I mean, he just wanted to draw,

79
00:04:40,236 --> 00:04:44,022
and he didn't understand why
that wasn't "cool," you know?

80
00:04:44,109 --> 00:04:46,677
I mean, niggas will
make fun of you for anything,

81
00:04:46,677 --> 00:04:49,245
and I don't think
Thomas was built to take it.

82
00:04:49,332 --> 00:04:51,595
I told him, "Son,

83
00:04:51,595 --> 00:04:56,513
one day, ain't none of this
gonna even matter."

84
00:05:12,007 --> 00:05:15,706
In 1987, Thomas left
East Atlanta to attend

85
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:19,101
Savannah College
of Art and Design, or SCAD.

86
00:05:20,276 --> 00:05:22,234
It was here that Thomas's

87
00:05:22,278 --> 00:05:25,673
creative dreams
began to take shape.

88
00:05:25,716 --> 00:05:28,850
We didn't have a lot of Black
students in the program then.

89
00:05:28,937 --> 00:05:32,157
Thomas showed a lot of promise.

90
00:05:32,201 --> 00:05:35,944
In his application,
he wrote about how he wanted

91
00:05:35,987 --> 00:05:38,425
to work at Disney one day.

92
00:05:38,425 --> 00:05:40,514
We usually laugh
at that sort of thing.

93
00:05:40,557 --> 00:05:42,733
Everybody says that, you know?

94
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:47,999
But with his application,
he sent a little flip-book.

95
00:05:47,999 --> 00:05:49,479
Can you see this?

96
00:05:53,091 --> 00:05:56,530
I mean, he had the sensibility

97
00:05:56,530 --> 00:06:00,795
and the wit
of a true Disney animator.

98
00:06:03,058 --> 00:06:07,497
Art Babbitt was a big name
at Disney Animation for a time.

99
00:06:07,541 --> 00:06:09,456
As an animator
in the '30s and '40s,

100
00:06:09,543 --> 00:06:12,110
he racked up a lot of credits,

101
00:06:12,154 --> 00:06:13,677
and enemies,

102
00:06:13,764 --> 00:06:15,897
but he was most well-known for

103
00:06:15,984 --> 00:06:18,247
developing
the character of Goofy.

104
00:06:18,247 --> 00:06:21,946
He came to SCAD, and he spoke.

105
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:39,311
Thomas really gravitated to him.

106
00:06:39,355 --> 00:06:42,576
He became obsessed,
and he really glommed

107
00:06:42,663 --> 00:06:46,362
on to Babbitt's article
"Analysis of the Goof."

108
00:06:48,146 --> 00:06:50,105
Art describes Goofy.

109
00:06:50,148 --> 00:06:52,368
He says, "Think of
the Goof as a composite

110
00:06:52,368 --> 00:06:56,372
"of an everlasting optimist,
a gullible Good Samaritan,

111
00:06:56,459 --> 00:07:00,158
"a half-wit, a shiftless,

112
00:07:00,158 --> 00:07:01,290
"good-natured colored boy,

113
00:07:01,377 --> 00:07:02,683
"and a hick.

114
00:07:02,726 --> 00:07:04,467
"He is loose-jointed

115
00:07:04,511 --> 00:07:06,904
"and gangly, but not rubbery.

116
00:07:06,948 --> 00:07:09,516
"He can move fast if he has to,
but would rather

117
00:07:09,603 --> 00:07:12,388
"avoid overexertion,

118
00:07:12,475 --> 00:07:15,391
"so he takes what seems
the easiest way.

119
00:07:15,478 --> 00:07:19,743
"He is a philosopher of
the barber shop variety.

120
00:07:19,830 --> 00:07:23,051
"No matter what happens,
he accepts it finally

121
00:07:23,094 --> 00:07:27,621
as being for the best
or at least amusing."

122
00:07:27,708 --> 00:07:32,626
Thomas used this text
as the center of a piece

123
00:07:32,669 --> 00:07:36,456
of a series he called
"Goofy, Please."

124
00:07:37,979 --> 00:07:41,417
After class one day, he
showed me some of these pieces,

125
00:07:41,504 --> 00:07:44,202
and I looked at it,
and I looked at him,

126
00:07:44,246 --> 00:07:45,987
and I thought,

127
00:07:46,074 --> 00:07:49,556
"This kid is from
another universe,

128
00:07:49,556 --> 00:07:52,559
like, a whole nother planet."

129
00:07:52,646 --> 00:07:55,300
Thomas would be like,
"Hey, come on,

130
00:07:55,344 --> 00:07:58,434
you know Goofy's a [bleep]."

131
00:07:58,434 --> 00:08:00,523
:
And we would just laugh, man.

132
00:08:00,567 --> 00:08:02,960
We just thought
that was so funny.

133
00:08:03,004 --> 00:08:06,790
It was clear that
Thomas was very talented.

134
00:08:06,877 --> 00:08:08,400
For his thesis,

135
00:08:08,444 --> 00:08:11,360
he made this
incredible short film.

136
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:15,320
♪ Well, I'd like to visit
the moon♪

137
00:08:15,364 --> 00:08:19,455
♪ On a rocket ship
high in the air...♪

138
00:08:19,499 --> 00:08:21,805
He had little screenings;

139
00:08:21,892 --> 00:08:24,329
teachers would come,
and students.

140
00:08:24,373 --> 00:08:28,246
The film felt so deep.

141
00:08:28,333 --> 00:08:30,684
I-I think it was somehow
connected to him

142
00:08:30,684 --> 00:08:32,860
losing his father
in freshman year.

143
00:08:32,903 --> 00:08:34,557
♪ I would miss
all the places and...♪

144
00:08:34,601 --> 00:08:36,124
Uncle Ronnie dying
changed him, man.

145
00:08:36,167 --> 00:08:39,040
Thomas was in his
freshman year at SCAD,

146
00:08:39,127 --> 00:08:41,042
and I didn't think he
was going to make it, but, uh,

147
00:08:41,129 --> 00:08:43,479
once we buried
my Uncle Ronnie, man,

148
00:08:43,566 --> 00:08:47,527
he, uh-- it's like
his brain exploded.

149
00:08:49,267 --> 00:08:53,054
Depending on who saw it
and when they saw it,

150
00:08:53,054 --> 00:08:56,274
it was either
the funniest thing,

151
00:08:56,361 --> 00:08:58,625
or the saddest thing
you'd ever seen.

152
00:08:58,712 --> 00:09:02,846
That short was what
got him into Disney.

153
00:09:02,846 --> 00:09:04,195
No question.

154
00:09:08,722 --> 00:09:11,725
Washington comes to Disney

155
00:09:11,812 --> 00:09:14,292
right out of college,
fresh from SCAD.

156
00:09:14,379 --> 00:09:18,166
You know, he's there,
the company's doing pretty well,

157
00:09:18,209 --> 00:09:19,646
they've just had their hit
with The Little Mermaid.

158
00:09:21,473 --> 00:09:24,302
One of the things that-that
I'm glad that I was

159
00:09:24,389 --> 00:09:26,261
able to be part of
was that, um,

160
00:09:26,304 --> 00:09:29,960
Disney instituted this
diversity program.

161
00:09:30,047 --> 00:09:34,530
We knew that we needed to
bring in younger voices

162
00:09:34,574 --> 00:09:37,185
from a diversified
point of view,

163
00:09:37,228 --> 00:09:39,317
and that would actually, uh,

164
00:09:39,361 --> 00:09:41,885
strengthen our company.

165
00:09:41,885 --> 00:09:45,062
And Thomas was one
of the first of those.

166
00:09:45,106 --> 00:09:48,718
Thomas started out at
Disney as an assistant animator

167
00:09:48,762 --> 00:09:52,330
on DuckTales the Movie:
Treasure of the Lost Lamp.

168
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:55,116
It wasn't necessarily
creative work,

169
00:09:55,203 --> 00:09:57,422
but things are fine, you know.

170
00:09:57,466 --> 00:10:00,512
It's not really moving
as fast as he wants things

171
00:10:00,556 --> 00:10:02,645
to be moving,
but he's doing all right.

172
00:10:02,689 --> 00:10:05,561
And then the uprisings happened.

173
00:10:11,654 --> 00:10:13,264
When the shooting happened,

174
00:10:13,351 --> 00:10:16,311
and then the riots,

175
00:10:16,354 --> 00:10:19,662
Thomas was working in L.A.

176
00:10:19,706 --> 00:10:23,710
He called me
and he said, "Mama,

177
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:27,278
I just want to go down there."

178
00:10:27,278 --> 00:10:30,891
And I told him,

179
00:10:30,934 --> 00:10:34,590
"Son, you need to riot

180
00:10:34,677 --> 00:10:36,244
with that pen and paper."

181
00:10:36,287 --> 00:10:38,594
He was shook.

182
00:10:38,594 --> 00:10:40,161
I could see it in him,
he was shook.

183
00:10:40,248 --> 00:10:42,380
He actually called me
one time and was like,

184
00:10:42,467 --> 00:10:45,166
"If I ever get the chance
to do a movie around here,

185
00:10:45,253 --> 00:10:47,777
I'm not holding back."

186
00:10:59,049 --> 00:11:01,138
While South Central Los Angeles

187
00:11:01,182 --> 00:11:03,401
was engulfed in flames,

188
00:11:03,488 --> 00:11:07,623
15 miles north in Burbank,
another fire was about to erup.

189
00:11:07,710 --> 00:11:11,453
What happened next is
one of the most unlikely

190
00:11:11,496 --> 00:11:15,718
and unprecedented events in the
history of corporate America.

191
00:11:15,762 --> 00:11:17,938
After the success
of Beauty and the Beast,

192
00:11:17,981 --> 00:11:20,941
Michael Eisner put
Dennis Stephenson in charge

193
00:11:20,984 --> 00:11:23,421
while he went on a sabbatical.

194
00:11:23,508 --> 00:11:26,076
But two weeks into the job,

195
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:28,513
Stephenson had
a health emergency

196
00:11:28,557 --> 00:11:30,907
and passed shortly thereafter...

197
00:11:32,648 --> 00:11:34,824
...leaving the job
open once more.

198
00:11:36,521 --> 00:11:38,523
We all voted for this guy

199
00:11:38,567 --> 00:11:39,786
named Tom Washington.

200
00:11:39,873 --> 00:11:41,570
But we didn't realize

201
00:11:41,657 --> 00:11:43,746
that Tom's first name

202
00:11:43,790 --> 00:11:48,229
was actually Thompson,
not Thomas.

203
00:11:48,316 --> 00:11:51,667
Thomas Washington
was an animator.

204
00:11:51,667 --> 00:11:54,670
People were pretty upset
when they found out

205
00:11:54,670 --> 00:11:55,802
they voted for the wrong man.

206
00:11:58,456 --> 00:12:00,589
It felt like an honest mistake
that we

207
00:12:00,589 --> 00:12:03,200
could sweep under the rug, but

208
00:12:03,244 --> 00:12:04,593
after Beauty and the Beast,

209
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,117
there were a lot of eyes on us.

210
00:12:07,161 --> 00:12:08,553
We tried to handle it smoothly,

211
00:12:08,597 --> 00:12:11,687
but Thomas held our feet
to the fire.

212
00:12:13,558 --> 00:12:17,040
So, because of this,
you know, "behind closed door,

213
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:19,564
boys' club" handshake,

214
00:12:19,608 --> 00:12:21,697
Disney is now in radical

215
00:12:21,697 --> 00:12:24,265
new territory
but entirely by default.

216
00:12:24,265 --> 00:12:26,833
So you have this young animato,

217
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:29,139
this young, Black animator

218
00:12:29,139 --> 00:12:32,490
who comes into this company,
all of a sudden has a ton

219
00:12:32,577 --> 00:12:36,059
of power at a hugely
culturally significant moment,

220
00:12:36,146 --> 00:12:38,061
and he wants
to do something with it.

221
00:12:38,148 --> 00:12:39,280
He wants to prove himself.

222
00:12:39,367 --> 00:12:41,282
This cat was on my line

223
00:12:41,369 --> 00:12:42,936
with some foolishness,

224
00:12:42,936 --> 00:12:44,502
talking about
he's the CEO of Disney.

225
00:12:44,502 --> 00:12:47,418
I was, I was crying laughing.

226
00:12:49,507 --> 00:12:53,511
And then, you know,
he took me to his office

227
00:12:53,598 --> 00:12:55,949
and people was,
like, greeting him.

228
00:12:55,949 --> 00:12:59,343
First day,
he took us all into a room

229
00:12:59,387 --> 00:13:01,302
that we call the stock room,
which is where everybody

230
00:13:01,302 --> 00:13:02,869
hangs out before
we get to work.

231
00:13:02,869 --> 00:13:05,872
And he shows us
a frame of animation

232
00:13:05,872 --> 00:13:10,746
of Goofy in Mickey's trailer,
and, uh,

233
00:13:10,746 --> 00:13:13,096
Mickey has Pluto on a leash.

234
00:13:13,183 --> 00:13:16,099
And it gets real quiet,

235
00:13:16,186 --> 00:13:18,406
and, uh, Washington goes,

236
00:13:18,449 --> 00:13:21,104
"Why is Goofy
letting Mickey do that?"

237
00:13:21,191 --> 00:13:23,280
He's a very fine dog, sir.

238
00:13:23,324 --> 00:13:25,630
And, you know, we're all
confused, we're just looking

239
00:13:25,674 --> 00:13:28,938
at each other, and Charlie goes,
"What do you mean, sir?"

240
00:13:28,982 --> 00:13:33,203
And Washington goes,
"Goofy's a dog,

241
00:13:33,203 --> 00:13:36,990
"and Pluto's a dog,
so why is he letting Mickey

242
00:13:37,033 --> 00:13:39,122
do that to one of his own?"

243
00:13:40,863 --> 00:13:44,127
Yeah, T had them
running scared from day one.

244
00:13:44,127 --> 00:13:48,566
I was, uh, fresh-faced
and wild-eyed.

245
00:13:48,653 --> 00:13:53,441
I was an inbetweener
for about three years,

246
00:13:53,484 --> 00:13:55,051
didn't see much movement.

247
00:13:57,358 --> 00:14:00,752
An inbetweener was usually
for animators just starting ou.

248
00:14:00,796 --> 00:14:04,887
So, say a character
slaps somebody across the face.

249
00:14:04,931 --> 00:14:08,456
They would get a senior artist
to do the fun part,

250
00:14:08,499 --> 00:14:13,896
drawing the first and the last
frame that define the movement.

251
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:18,292
The inbetweener draws all the
many little frames in between.

252
00:14:19,815 --> 00:14:21,730
I was stuck as an inbetweener

253
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:23,819
for years and years,

254
00:14:23,819 --> 00:14:25,952
but as soon as Thomas came in,
he said,

255
00:14:26,039 --> 00:14:28,606
"I want you to be
lead director on this."

256
00:14:29,912 --> 00:14:33,350
And I said, "On what?"
And he said,

257
00:14:33,394 --> 00:14:36,092
"The Blackest movie
of all time."

258
00:14:37,833 --> 00:14:39,835
-♪ Check, check, uh♪
-♪ Get it, get, get...♪

259
00:14:39,922 --> 00:14:43,970
He said he wanted to make
a movie about Black fatherhood.

260
00:14:43,970 --> 00:14:46,233
♪ Get, get, get it...♪

261
00:14:46,276 --> 00:14:48,713
Man, he wanted to tackle
everything in this movie.

262
00:14:48,757 --> 00:14:52,630
Segregation, single parenting,

263
00:14:52,630 --> 00:14:54,850
low-income career trajectories,

264
00:14:54,937 --> 00:14:58,419
fear of gang violence, incarceration,

265
00:14:58,419 --> 00:15:02,640
the amount of cheese
in African-American diets.

266
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:07,297
He wanted to make a movie that
covered all this and more...

267
00:15:07,384 --> 00:15:10,431
and he said he knew
the perfect character to do it.

268
00:15:10,431 --> 00:15:12,389
♪ Swingin' while I'm singin'♪

269
00:15:12,433 --> 00:15:13,869
♪ Hey!♪

270
00:15:13,869 --> 00:15:18,221
Thomas, man,
he knew that people thought

271
00:15:18,308 --> 00:15:21,094
Goofy was dumb,
but he wanted to show

272
00:15:21,094 --> 00:15:24,097
the systemic factors
that Goofy was dealing with.

273
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:27,404
A shitty job, angry kid,

274
00:15:27,448 --> 00:15:30,233
and embarrassed
by his lack of influence.

275
00:15:31,539 --> 00:15:36,457
Man, it was wild how deep
he leaned in to all this,

276
00:15:36,544 --> 00:15:38,676
'cause, uh, as far as I knew,

277
00:15:38,763 --> 00:15:42,463
Thomas had a really solid homelife.

278
00:15:42,463 --> 00:15:45,466
It's a vacation with me
and my best buddy.

279
00:15:45,466 --> 00:15:46,554
Oh, Donald Duck?

280
00:15:46,597 --> 00:15:49,209
No, silly, with you!

281
00:16:02,570 --> 00:16:05,834
Thomas and I,
we were married young.

282
00:16:05,921 --> 00:16:09,011
We didn't have much,

283
00:16:09,055 --> 00:16:12,232
and, um, you know what, he nevr

284
00:16:12,275 --> 00:16:16,018
let that get
in the way of us, though.

285
00:16:16,062 --> 00:16:17,454
If there was something
we couldn't afford,

286
00:16:17,498 --> 00:16:19,587
he would just draw it.

287
00:16:19,630 --> 00:16:22,938
You know,
you'd wake up and I would

288
00:16:22,938 --> 00:16:27,725
see anything from a coffee makr
to a solid-gold jet

289
00:16:27,725 --> 00:16:31,294
on the refrigerator
in the morning.

290
00:16:31,294 --> 00:16:32,774
That was Thomas.

291
00:16:34,167 --> 00:16:38,040
We-we had our son Maxwell
pretty early.

292
00:16:38,084 --> 00:16:40,086
He and Thomas,
they were inseparable.

293
00:16:40,173 --> 00:16:42,175
He did everything for that boy.

294
00:16:42,218 --> 00:16:45,265
Oh, my God, look at this...

295
00:16:45,308 --> 00:16:46,962
Oh, look at this!

296
00:16:47,006 --> 00:16:50,096
Thomas kept saying that

297
00:16:50,183 --> 00:16:52,315
A Goofy Movie was for him.

298
00:16:53,664 --> 00:16:58,408
He'd say, "If I'm not here,
you can show him this."

299
00:16:58,452 --> 00:17:02,630
I was too scared to ask him
what he meant by that.

300
00:17:02,630 --> 00:17:05,850
To give you a little context,
when A Goofy Movie came out,

301
00:17:05,894 --> 00:17:09,376
Black masculinity in the media
was in a really weird place.

302
00:17:09,419 --> 00:17:13,554
- Hello, I'm Blaine Edwards.
- And I'm Antoine Merriweather!

303
00:17:13,641 --> 00:17:15,991
And welcome to Men on Films.

304
00:17:16,078 --> 00:17:17,775
The show that looks at movies..

305
00:17:17,862 --> 00:17:20,213
...from a male point of view.

306
00:17:20,300 --> 00:17:23,216
So on one hand,
you've got men doing, kind of

307
00:17:23,303 --> 00:17:26,306
"faux queer" comedy,
and on the other end

308
00:17:26,349 --> 00:17:29,135
of the spectrum, you've got
these hypermasculine portrayals.

309
00:17:31,833 --> 00:17:34,575
Am I my brother's keeper?

310
00:17:36,707 --> 00:17:38,187
Yes, I am.

311
00:17:40,798 --> 00:17:43,366
And, by contrast, Goofy

312
00:17:43,453 --> 00:17:46,587
is a nuanced portrayal
of a Black man

313
00:17:46,587 --> 00:17:49,677
whose priority is his family.

314
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:52,810
- What?
- Nothin'.

315
00:17:52,810 --> 00:17:56,118
Goofy really put forward
the philosophy that...

316
00:17:56,162 --> 00:18:01,254
just love your children for
who they are and accept them,

317
00:18:01,254 --> 00:18:03,038
and deal with the pain
that the world

318
00:18:03,038 --> 00:18:04,170
is not going to love them
like you do.

319
00:18:12,961 --> 00:18:17,052
I think in the movie,
Dad laid in a lot of things.

320
00:18:17,052 --> 00:18:20,360
I-I notice it more now
than I did then.

321
00:18:20,403 --> 00:18:22,057
♪ If I could make you
stop and take a look♪

322
00:18:22,144 --> 00:18:23,580
♪ At me instead of just...♪

323
00:18:23,624 --> 00:18:28,019
In the beginning,
Max sings a song,

324
00:18:28,063 --> 00:18:31,458
dunks a basketball, and then
is cheered on by his peers.

325
00:18:33,286 --> 00:18:35,288
I think my dad
was making a comment

326
00:18:35,331 --> 00:18:36,854
on Black exceptionalism

327
00:18:36,854 --> 00:18:40,075
and how some people believe

328
00:18:40,075 --> 00:18:44,732
that performative assimilation
is the best way to get by.

329
00:18:44,819 --> 00:18:49,171
My dad wanted to prove those
limitations were just that,

330
00:18:49,215 --> 00:18:52,609
limits put on us by outsiders.

331
00:18:52,653 --> 00:18:56,744
That's why he made it
a fishing trip.

332
00:18:56,744 --> 00:18:59,138
It's the same one
we used to take. [chuckles]

333
00:19:01,183 --> 00:19:03,446
The trip was
supposed to start out

334
00:19:03,446 --> 00:19:06,493
as sort of like a freedom ride.

335
00:19:06,536 --> 00:19:08,930
And the map,
which is central to the plot

336
00:19:08,973 --> 00:19:13,674
of the film and also representd
Goofy's trust in his son Max,

337
00:19:13,674 --> 00:19:16,285
was supposed to
remind the viewer

338
00:19:16,329 --> 00:19:18,983
of the Green Book
and how Black travelers

339
00:19:19,027 --> 00:19:21,508
had to use it
to advance their way

340
00:19:21,551 --> 00:19:25,555
through the Jim Crow South
during the '30s and the '40s.

341
00:19:25,599 --> 00:19:29,559
A lot of the film had
deeper meanings, and word

342
00:19:29,646 --> 00:19:34,434
got around that it meant
a lot more in Thomas's eyes.

343
00:19:34,477 --> 00:19:37,872
Dad wanted every Black
person in America to feel like

344
00:19:37,915 --> 00:19:42,572
they were just a road trip away
from being part of a movement.

345
00:19:42,616 --> 00:19:46,359
Even if their kids
didn't understand the stakes.

346
00:19:46,446 --> 00:19:50,363
It was about destiny
and how our children,

347
00:19:50,406 --> 00:19:53,366
our future,
get to draw our path.

348
00:19:53,453 --> 00:19:55,368
I want to get us
to doing something

349
00:19:55,411 --> 00:19:56,717
we've never done before.

350
00:19:56,804 --> 00:19:58,806
We have never truly drawn

351
00:19:58,893 --> 00:20:02,636
authentically Black characters,
characters that act...

352
00:20:08,163 --> 00:20:10,731
Now, see,
this is what I'm talking about.

353
00:20:10,818 --> 00:20:13,734
This is what I'm talking about.

354
00:20:13,821 --> 00:20:17,172
In order to draw
authentically Black characters,

355
00:20:17,259 --> 00:20:19,305
one has to know Black people.

356
00:20:19,392 --> 00:20:23,831
Okay? Now,
Tom went out on assignment.

357
00:20:23,874 --> 00:20:27,617
Researched, hung out,
went to a couple Black cookouts.

358
00:20:27,661 --> 00:20:31,969
Said the wrong thing,
got his ass beat, but now, now?

359
00:20:31,969 --> 00:20:34,537
He's more aware,
he's more knowledgeable

360
00:20:34,624 --> 00:20:36,496
of how to draw. Okay?

361
00:20:36,539 --> 00:20:39,716
Thomas tried to pull back
and be more

362
00:20:39,760 --> 00:20:43,416
subtle with his message,
uh, when the complaints started,

363
00:20:43,416 --> 00:20:45,766
and we were getting complaints
from everywhere.

364
00:20:45,766 --> 00:20:51,206
But there was
nothing subtle about Thomas.

365
00:20:51,206 --> 00:20:53,904
At one point, he said he
didn't want Mickey in the movie.

366
00:20:53,904 --> 00:20:56,429
And, um, I kept saying,

367
00:20:56,429 --> 00:20:58,779
"But it's Disney.
What are you talking about?"

368
00:20:58,779 --> 00:21:01,869
I can remember
Thomas ranting in his office,

369
00:21:01,869 --> 00:21:04,175
"They're trying to make me
put this white boy in my movie."

370
00:21:04,219 --> 00:21:07,788
And I'm thinking,
"He's a mouse."

371
00:21:07,875 --> 00:21:10,094
He told me from the beginning,

372
00:21:10,138 --> 00:21:13,010
"I'm not here for a long time,
I'm here for a good time."

373
00:21:13,097 --> 00:21:16,449
Uh, he knew
he was going to get fired,

374
00:21:16,536 --> 00:21:19,365
but the "when" of it
was fun for him.

375
00:21:19,452 --> 00:21:21,236
It was like a game.

376
00:21:21,323 --> 00:21:26,894
We had 15, 16,
sometimes 18-hour days.

377
00:21:26,894 --> 00:21:30,593
And, uh, he would be screaming,
"We don't dance like that."

378
00:21:30,593 --> 00:21:34,380
And he would
bring his friend's kids in

379
00:21:34,467 --> 00:21:37,470
and, uh,
they would moonwalk and,

380
00:21:37,470 --> 00:21:40,777
and, you know, do all, like,
you know, this stuff, you know?

381
00:21:40,821 --> 00:21:42,823
Oh, man,
I must have drawn a pair

382
00:21:42,910 --> 00:21:46,479
of gloves dapping,
I don't know, over 5,000 times?

383
00:21:46,479 --> 00:21:49,003
I just kept hearing, "That's
not a dap, that's not a dap.

384
00:21:49,046 --> 00:21:50,134
Where's the snap?"

385
00:21:50,178 --> 00:21:51,571
You see my knees?

386
00:21:51,614 --> 00:21:54,443
You see this,
see this angle of these knees?

387
00:21:54,487 --> 00:21:57,054
Draw-- Draw this!
Get this down, okay?

388
00:21:57,141 --> 00:21:59,230
One time,
I just couldn't take it--

389
00:21:59,274 --> 00:22:02,103
my fingers were just bleeding--
so I went up to talk to him,

390
00:22:02,146 --> 00:22:04,497
and he was up there drinking
whiskey with Robert Townsend,

391
00:22:04,584 --> 00:22:08,501
and Janet Jackson, uh,
Sinbad and Adina Howard.

392
00:22:08,588 --> 00:22:11,721
Well, when I poked
my head in, Thomas went,

393
00:22:11,765 --> 00:22:13,288
"Who ordered the white rice?"

394
00:22:13,332 --> 00:22:15,638
And they all just laughed,

395
00:22:15,725 --> 00:22:19,076
and, uh, I just didn't get it.

396
00:22:19,076 --> 00:22:20,817
BRIAN McKNIGHT:
Everybody used to be up there.

397
00:22:20,861 --> 00:22:24,734
Eddie Murphy,
Arsenio Hall, Kadeem Hardison,

398
00:22:24,778 --> 00:22:28,738
Harrison Ford, it was crazy.
The parties were insane.

399
00:22:28,782 --> 00:22:31,959
Imagine Disney after 
The Lion King.

400
00:22:32,002 --> 00:22:34,701
You got this smooth-looking
brother at the helm.

401
00:22:38,661 --> 00:22:40,968
It was crazy
he even had that office.

402
00:22:41,011 --> 00:22:42,622
Everyone would go
up there and just hang

403
00:22:42,665 --> 00:22:45,625
as we plotted our overthrow
of Hollywood.

404
00:22:45,668 --> 00:22:47,453
You couldn't tell us nothing.

405
00:22:47,453 --> 00:22:49,890
They had a big
musical number planned

406
00:22:49,890 --> 00:22:52,196
with Bryan Adams in the movie.

407
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:55,461
And one day,
Thomas asked me to come by

408
00:22:55,461 --> 00:22:57,898
and see what he's working on.

409
00:22:57,898 --> 00:23:00,074
And we're walking through,
and he stops in one

410
00:23:00,117 --> 00:23:01,902
of the rooms and plays me
some of the music.

411
00:23:01,989 --> 00:23:05,122
And I'm like,
"Yeah, you know, pop ballad.

412
00:23:05,209 --> 00:23:06,646
"You know, same old, same old.

413
00:23:06,689 --> 00:23:09,518
You know, it's-it's good,
it's-it's cool."

414
00:23:09,562 --> 00:23:12,869
But he asks me,
"Who do you like right now?"

415
00:23:12,913 --> 00:23:15,045
I'm like, "You know,
I-I've been listening

416
00:23:15,132 --> 00:23:17,221
to this Tevin Campbell."

417
00:23:17,265 --> 00:23:21,704
And from then on, it just
had to be Tevin Campbell.

418
00:23:21,791 --> 00:23:26,709
♪ Last night I, I saw you♪

419
00:23:26,709 --> 00:23:28,058
♪ Standing♪

420
00:23:28,058 --> 00:23:30,365
♪ And I started♪

421
00:23:30,409 --> 00:23:32,715
♪ Started pretending♪

422
00:23:32,715 --> 00:23:34,500
Most of the cats
around there didn't

423
00:23:34,587 --> 00:23:36,371
even know who
Tevin Campbell was.

424
00:23:36,458 --> 00:23:40,810
For three months, a lot of them
were saying Kevin Campbell.

425
00:23:40,810 --> 00:23:45,728
♪ Can we talk, baby,
can we talk for a minute?♪

426
00:23:45,815 --> 00:23:47,948
♪ Girl, I want
to know your name♪

427
00:23:47,948 --> 00:23:50,341
♪ I want to know your name♪

428
00:23:50,385 --> 00:23:51,952
Thomas was a cool cat.

429
00:23:52,039 --> 00:23:54,824
But the brother definitely had
a chip on his shoulder.

430
00:23:54,824 --> 00:23:56,435
He didn't like it if
you challenged his Blackness.

431
00:23:59,612 --> 00:24:01,701
I think there was
a part of Thomas

432
00:24:01,744 --> 00:24:05,531
that kind of felt that
being an animator

433
00:24:05,531 --> 00:24:08,534
wasn't innately Black, you know?

434
00:24:08,534 --> 00:24:11,537
And he wanted to make it Black,

435
00:24:11,537 --> 00:24:13,277
he wanted to make it cool.

436
00:24:13,321 --> 00:24:17,368
He wanted to-to carve out
his own space and not feel

437
00:24:17,412 --> 00:24:20,981
that he was divorced
from his own culture, you know.

438
00:24:21,068 --> 00:24:24,506
He was loading his culture
into this stuff that

439
00:24:24,550 --> 00:24:26,987
we were doing so that
it just seemed normal

440
00:24:27,074 --> 00:24:30,817
and would seem normal
to the rest of us.

441
00:24:30,860 --> 00:24:33,994
He had a lot of pressure, man--
the culture, the job itself,

442
00:24:33,994 --> 00:24:36,866
what-what he was trying to live
up to that I'm not even sure

443
00:24:36,866 --> 00:24:38,781
that he knew that he could
live up to it.

444
00:24:38,781 --> 00:24:42,742
And, uh, it had to
take a huge toll on him.

445
00:24:42,785 --> 00:24:44,961
One morning I walk in
the studio, I got my coffee,

446
00:24:45,005 --> 00:24:47,398
I'm ready to sit down,
get to busi-- business.

447
00:24:47,442 --> 00:24:50,750
And Thomas like, "Psst! Psst!
Come here, come here!"

448
00:24:50,793 --> 00:24:53,666
So I go over there and, uh,
he's in his cubicle,

449
00:24:53,709 --> 00:24:57,234
he got this briefcase,
he opens it up,

450
00:24:57,278 --> 00:24:58,801
there's a gun inside!

451
00:24:58,801 --> 00:25:03,240
I'm like,
"Dude, what-what is this?

452
00:25:03,284 --> 00:25:05,112
What's it for?"

453
00:25:05,155 --> 00:25:09,682
And he's like,
"This is to remind me that

454
00:25:09,682 --> 00:25:11,945
they don't know
who the fuck I am."

455
00:25:17,690 --> 00:25:21,128
Dad kind of started to lose it.

456
00:25:21,171 --> 00:25:24,044
He would stay up all night,
talk to hisself.

457
00:25:24,131 --> 00:25:28,309
After a while, Mom...
she had had enough.

458
00:25:30,920 --> 00:25:33,488
After a while,
Mom couldn't take it anymore.

459
00:25:33,488 --> 00:25:35,272
He had just gotten too mean.

460
00:25:37,187 --> 00:25:39,712
We had so much fun.

461
00:25:40,626 --> 00:25:44,717
But then, it wasn't fun anymor.

462
00:25:44,804 --> 00:25:47,284
He had been
fooling around, yeah.

463
00:25:47,371 --> 00:25:51,288
And drinking a lot,
and he got pretty mean.

464
00:25:51,375 --> 00:25:54,291
He would say some mean,
nasty things to me,

465
00:25:54,378 --> 00:25:57,512
which he never,
he had never done that before.

466
00:26:00,036 --> 00:26:02,038
I remember at one time,
Maxwell tried to step in

467
00:26:02,082 --> 00:26:03,300
between his dad and I,

468
00:26:03,344 --> 00:26:06,303
and Thomas...

469
00:26:06,390 --> 00:26:10,090
was not kind.
He kind of...

470
00:26:12,309 --> 00:26:15,182
He kind of raised his, um...

471
00:26:15,225 --> 00:26:18,228
he raised his fist...

472
00:26:18,315 --> 00:26:20,883
at Maxwell.

473
00:26:20,970 --> 00:26:24,191
That wasn't cool,
because they-they

474
00:26:24,234 --> 00:26:25,801
had always been so close.

475
00:26:28,804 --> 00:26:30,676
He was a...

476
00:26:30,763 --> 00:26:33,113
he was a really good father.

477
00:26:33,200 --> 00:26:36,116
I mean, at least he tried
to be a good father.

478
00:26:36,116 --> 00:26:39,075
I kept saying, "Thomas,

479
00:26:39,119 --> 00:26:41,904
"you're going to lose him,

480
00:26:41,904 --> 00:26:44,820
you're going to lose your son."

481
00:26:47,344 --> 00:26:50,913
But all he kept saying is...

482
00:26:50,913 --> 00:26:53,046
"But this is for him."

483
00:26:55,918 --> 00:26:58,660
I knew things
were going downhill when

484
00:26:58,704 --> 00:27:00,793
Thomas's wife left him.

485
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:03,056
Sh-She had been there
from the beginning.

486
00:27:04,361 --> 00:27:06,929
And he started
sleeping in his office.

487
00:27:07,016 --> 00:27:11,020
And, um, one day,
there was a board meeting.

488
00:27:11,107 --> 00:27:15,764
All the Disney heads were ther,
and they started talking

489
00:27:15,808 --> 00:27:19,550
about the budget was
going a bit high on Goofy.

490
00:27:19,594 --> 00:27:22,684
And one of the guys
asked Thomas, he said, uh,

491
00:27:22,728 --> 00:27:24,904
"Are you in control
of the budget?"

492
00:27:24,947 --> 00:27:26,819
And Thomas said,
"Of course I am.

493
00:27:26,862 --> 00:27:28,908
I'm Goofy."

494
00:27:28,951 --> 00:27:30,692
And then,

495
00:27:30,736 --> 00:27:35,479
he did this this chilling laug. Like...

496
00:27:39,092 --> 00:27:42,617
You know, l-like Goofy,
but broken.

497
00:27:43,966 --> 00:27:46,534
:
And it-it was terrifying, man.

498
00:27:46,534 --> 00:27:48,101
I almost started crying.

499
00:27:50,407 --> 00:27:52,758
He really thought he was Goofy.

500
00:27:58,981 --> 00:28:01,070
The company knew he had to go,

501
00:28:01,114 --> 00:28:04,857
but legally,
he was still the CEO.

502
00:28:04,900 --> 00:28:08,556
That gentleman's
show of hands was, uh,

503
00:28:08,643 --> 00:28:12,647
surprisingly hard to go back o.

504
00:28:12,734 --> 00:28:15,084
You know, they even tried to
buy him out of his contract.

505
00:28:15,128 --> 00:28:18,784
Yeah, they offered him,
like $75,000.

506
00:28:18,871 --> 00:28:20,786
No, no.

507
00:28:20,873 --> 00:28:24,615
We offered him $75 million,

508
00:28:24,659 --> 00:28:27,096
paid out over ten years.

509
00:28:27,183 --> 00:28:32,014
Um, that's how... that's how
serious it was for us.

510
00:28:32,101 --> 00:28:33,842
I mean, he didn't take it.

511
00:28:33,886 --> 00:28:37,585
But, uh, maybe he
should have taken it, you know?

512
00:28:39,892 --> 00:28:43,460
He started
getting really paranoid.

513
00:28:43,547 --> 00:28:46,986
He'd say he was
afraid for his life,

514
00:28:47,029 --> 00:28:49,118
but he'd never say from whom.

515
00:28:50,424 --> 00:28:52,687
Suddenly, there were
a bunch of guys in bow ties

516
00:28:52,774 --> 00:28:54,776
hanging around the office.

517
00:28:54,820 --> 00:28:56,691
He introduced us
to one of them saying,

518
00:28:56,778 --> 00:28:59,041
"I want you to meet my new
head of security,

519
00:28:59,041 --> 00:29:02,044
Al Shaheim X."

520
00:29:02,044 --> 00:29:04,699
The thing not a lot of people
knew about,

521
00:29:04,699 --> 00:29:07,267
was Thomas had
been going around town

522
00:29:07,354 --> 00:29:12,141
letting a lot of people know
how huge this was going to be,

523
00:29:12,141 --> 00:29:15,492
and he had a lot of different
Black empowerment groups,

524
00:29:15,492 --> 00:29:20,715
radical movements, and just
straight-up gangs all wanting

525
00:29:20,802 --> 00:29:25,154
as much influence and subtle
shout-outs as the other.

526
00:29:25,198 --> 00:29:27,940
You know,
I think that he had made

527
00:29:27,983 --> 00:29:29,985
way too many deals at one poin.

528
00:29:31,291 --> 00:29:34,250
At least, that's what...
that's what I heard.

529
00:29:34,294 --> 00:29:40,300
Truthfully, it all came down
to the end of the movie.

530
00:29:40,343 --> 00:29:42,345
Really.

531
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,698
Oh, man, the end of the movie.

532
00:29:47,742 --> 00:29:49,657
I heard he wanted Goofy
and Max to get pulled over

533
00:29:49,744 --> 00:29:52,486
early on and, uh, you know...

534
00:29:56,751 --> 00:29:59,885
Wh-Why would he do that?
That makes no sense.

535
00:30:01,190 --> 00:30:04,411
That Powerline concert
at the end,

536
00:30:04,411 --> 00:30:07,196
it wasn't supposed to be
all hunky-dory, dancing-dancing.

537
00:30:07,240 --> 00:30:09,677
Nah, it wasn't supposed
to be like that at all.

538
00:30:12,288 --> 00:30:15,204
I heard Goofy was supposed
to get shot at the concert.

539
00:30:15,248 --> 00:30:17,990
You know,
a Black man runs onstage

540
00:30:18,033 --> 00:30:20,557
like that at a major venue?
Come on, man.

541
00:30:20,644 --> 00:30:22,646
There's something wrong here.

542
00:30:22,690 --> 00:30:24,561
Thomas was trying
to make a statement.

543
00:30:26,302 --> 00:30:29,262
Do you, uh,
think his death was an accident?

544
00:30:31,133 --> 00:30:32,787
Next question, man.

545
00:30:32,831 --> 00:30:34,745
No, I don't.

546
00:30:34,789 --> 00:30:37,009
I-I've never thought
it was an accident.

547
00:30:38,445 --> 00:30:40,316
Why not?

548
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:42,797
Um, well...

549
00:30:44,146 --> 00:30:46,235
...partly because of
his-his mood at the end,

550
00:30:46,279 --> 00:30:48,934
but, um, you know,
there was also the tape.

551
00:30:50,500 --> 00:30:51,806
What tape?

552
00:31:00,684 --> 00:31:03,949
♪ For the first time ever♪

553
00:31:04,036 --> 00:31:06,952
♪ Seeing it eye to eye♪

554
00:31:08,605 --> 00:31:13,610
Oh, I'm so sorry.

555
00:31:13,610 --> 00:31:15,612
Oh, man, I...

556
00:31:15,699 --> 00:31:21,488
Ooh, I'm close, I'm-I'm close...

557
00:31:21,531 --> 00:31:23,664
I'm so close.

558
00:31:37,199 --> 00:31:39,419
I got to finish this.

559
00:31:44,859 --> 00:31:47,122
:
I got to finish it.

560
00:31:50,299 --> 00:31:54,086
Back then, mental health wasn't
something that we talked about.

561
00:31:54,173 --> 00:31:56,653
I didn't even know
what to characterize

562
00:31:56,653 --> 00:32:00,179
what I saw him going through,
what it... what it was.

563
00:32:00,222 --> 00:32:03,051
I just knew he was going
through something, um,

564
00:32:03,095 --> 00:32:06,011
something that was very, very
difficult for him to deal with.

565
00:32:08,230 --> 00:32:10,885
This is a little hard
for me right now.

566
00:32:13,801 --> 00:32:16,543
I-I don't know why.

567
00:32:16,630 --> 00:32:21,330
When the news finally broke
that Dad was officially out,

568
00:32:21,330 --> 00:32:24,681
they let him back on the lot
for one final screening

569
00:32:24,768 --> 00:32:27,032
of what they were able to
piece together behind his back.

570
00:32:29,991 --> 00:32:31,123
He didn't know they
had been working

571
00:32:31,166 --> 00:32:32,689
on the movie without him...

572
00:32:32,776 --> 00:32:35,040
changing and corrupting
his original vision.

573
00:32:36,171 --> 00:32:39,566
He-he didn't take the news well.

574
00:32:39,609 --> 00:32:41,568
I was at that last screening.

575
00:32:41,655 --> 00:32:44,353
It was the Bigfoot stuff
that put him over the edge.

576
00:32:44,440 --> 00:32:46,921
They'd added it
without his knowledge.

577
00:32:46,921 --> 00:32:48,923
He'd wanted Goofy and Max

578
00:32:48,923 --> 00:32:51,273
to wander into a thrift store

579
00:32:51,360 --> 00:32:54,320
and discover
Huey Newton's rattan throne.

580
00:32:54,363 --> 00:32:57,671
And once they sat in it,
Max would finally understand

581
00:32:57,714 --> 00:32:59,238
what Goofy had been fighting

582
00:32:59,281 --> 00:33:01,718
to make him understand
all along.

583
00:33:01,718 --> 00:33:06,593
Instead, they took out
the thrift store and the throne

584
00:33:06,636 --> 00:33:09,726
and put in that Bigfoot bit.

585
00:33:09,726 --> 00:33:12,686
It's like they were saying,
"What you were after,

586
00:33:12,729 --> 00:33:16,298
"your message,
it's a fantasy as elusive

587
00:33:16,298 --> 00:33:18,213
and unreal as Bigfoot."

588
00:33:19,519 --> 00:33:21,042
You're going to see...

589
00:33:21,086 --> 00:33:23,001
you're going to be
proud of me...

590
00:33:24,872 --> 00:33:27,266
I'm doing this for you.

591
00:33:27,309 --> 00:33:29,746
I'm doing this for all of us.

592
00:33:32,749 --> 00:33:34,403
Oh, man.

593
00:33:36,449 --> 00:33:40,540
I could feel him
get up and leave

594
00:33:40,627 --> 00:33:43,412
and I-I heard the door close
and I went out after him,

595
00:33:43,456 --> 00:33:46,546
and by the time I got
to the parking lot, he was gone.

596
00:33:46,546 --> 00:33:49,549
Something... I don't know,
there was a look in his eye.

597
00:33:49,549 --> 00:33:52,552
He-he just got in the car
and he took off...

598
00:33:53,857 --> 00:33:55,163
...and didn't come back.

599
00:34:16,010 --> 00:34:18,143
They, um...

600
00:34:18,230 --> 00:34:20,493
they never found his body.

601
00:34:26,455 --> 00:34:31,286
I think that was...
especially hard for Mom.

602
00:34:34,594 --> 00:34:35,725
Dad!

603
00:34:39,686 --> 00:34:42,036
We actually watch...

604
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:43,603
we watch the movies a lot.

605
00:34:43,690 --> 00:34:46,910
Uh, I couldn't for a while.

606
00:34:46,954 --> 00:34:52,046
But... but once I did,
I couldn't stop.

607
00:34:56,181 --> 00:34:57,965
It's like, um,

608
00:34:57,965 --> 00:35:00,402
it was like having him around,

609
00:35:00,489 --> 00:35:03,275
you know, in a fun way.

610
00:35:03,318 --> 00:35:04,667
Yeah.

611
00:35:14,286 --> 00:35:18,333
You know that, uh, "Damn,
you live like this?" meme?

612
00:35:19,639 --> 00:35:23,164
It's so funny to me
because the essence of it

613
00:35:23,208 --> 00:35:26,167
is those drawings
he did in college.

614
00:35:26,211 --> 00:35:30,171
Black folks living
their lives, being funny,

615
00:35:30,215 --> 00:35:34,654
being free, being real.

616
00:35:38,875 --> 00:35:41,443
He would've
been really proud that

617
00:35:41,530 --> 00:35:43,228
he became part of the culture.

618
00:35:44,446 --> 00:35:46,187
I think that's
what he wanted in the end,

619
00:35:46,231 --> 00:35:48,450
to just be part of the family.

620
00:35:51,366 --> 00:35:53,107
And along the way...

621
00:35:54,543 --> 00:35:57,546
...he made the Blackest
movie of all time.

622
00:36:04,727 --> 00:36:07,121
♪ Yeah, yeah♪

623
00:36:12,779 --> 00:36:15,564
♪ Got myself a notion♪

624
00:36:15,608 --> 00:36:20,482
♪ One I know
that you'll understand♪

625
00:36:20,569 --> 00:36:23,181
♪ To set the world in motion♪

626
00:36:24,486 --> 00:36:28,011
♪ By reaching out
for each other's hands♪

627
00:36:28,055 --> 00:36:32,625
♪ Maybe we'll discover What we
should have known all along♪

628
00:36:32,625 --> 00:36:35,715
♪ Yeah♪

629
00:36:35,802 --> 00:36:37,151
♪ One way or another♪

630
00:36:37,238 --> 00:36:40,372
♪ Together's
where we both belong♪

631
00:36:40,459 --> 00:36:43,723
♪ If we listen to
each other's heart♪

632
00:36:43,723 --> 00:36:45,377
♪ Oh, yeah♪

633
00:36:45,377 --> 00:36:47,379
♪ We'll find we're never
too far apart♪

634
00:36:47,422 --> 00:36:51,165
♪ And maybe love
is the reason why♪

635
00:36:51,252 --> 00:36:56,214
♪ For the first time ever,
we're seeing it eye to eye.♪

636
00:37:02,045 --> 00:37:03,743
